-- How do I get started in Google Ads?
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Describe what you are actually buying when you buy search advertising', 'Get an account open and billable before promising anyone a campaign', 'Decide whether search advertising suits a given business at all'],
  notes = 'Google Ads sells you the moment someone searches. You bid for a search phrase, and when it is typed your advert may appear. You pay when someone clicks, not when they see it.

WHY IT IS DIFFERENT FROM SOCIAL. You are not persuading someone who was doing something else. They typed a question. That intent is why search clicks cost more and convert better, and it is the whole reason to use it.

THE AUCTION IS NOT JUST MONEY. Position depends on what you bid AND on how relevant and useful your advert and its destination are. A well-matched advert pointing at a page that answers the search can beat a higher bid. This is why writing and landing pages matter here, not just budget.

THE STRUCTURE you will meet: ACCOUNT holds CAMPAIGNS, which set budget and location; campaigns hold AD GROUPS, which each cover one tight theme; ad groups hold KEYWORDS and ADS. The commonest beginner error is one ad group holding forty unrelated keywords, so no advert can be relevant to any of them.

WHEN IT IS WORTH IT: people actively search for what the business sells, a customer is worth more than a few clicks cost, and the destination can actually convert. When any of those is false, the money belongs elsewhere.

ADDITION BEYOND THE VIDEO — settle the practical barriers before you promise anything. BILLING: the account needs a valid payment method, usually a card, and billing is in a chosen currency; sort this out with the client first, because it stops more Kenyan campaigns at the start than any skill gap. NEVER RUN A CLIENT''S ADS ON YOUR PERSONAL ACCOUNT — the client should own the account and add you to it, so the history, the data and the billing stay theirs when the work ends. Doing it the other way is how people end up holding someone else''s payment liability and losing all the campaign history when the relationship ends.',
  practice_activity = 'Open an account and take it to the point where it could spend - billing added, one campaign drafted, not launched. Write down every barrier you hit and how long each took. Then assess one real business honestly against the three conditions and write whether search advertising is right for it, with your reason.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "What decides ad position besides the bid?", "options": ["How relevant and useful the advert and its destination are", "The size of the daily budget", "How long the account has existed", "The number of keywords in the ad group"], "correct_index": 0}, {"question": "Whose account should a client''s ads run in?", "options": ["The client''s own, with the manager added to it, so history and billing stay theirs", "The manager''s personal account, for convenience", "A shared account owned by neither", "Whichever has a working payment method"], "correct_index": 0}]'::jsonb
where id = '4f03155e-a753-4d32-9098-af457a0c4b33';

-- The BEST Facebook Ads Tutorial for Beginners (Full Guide)
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Explain how paid social differs from search and when to use it', 'Set up a campaign with a goal that matches the business result', 'Recognise what the creative has to do on an interruption channel'],
  notes = 'Paid social interrupts. Nobody was looking for the business, so the advert must earn attention before it can sell anything. That single difference from search changes everything about how it is built.

CHOOSE THE OBJECTIVE THAT MATCHES THE RESULT. The platform optimises hard for whatever you ask for, and it will deliver exactly that, which is the trap. Ask for engagement and you get likes from people who will never buy. Ask for messages and you get conversations. Choose the objective closest to money, always.

AUDIENCE. You can target by place, age, interests and behaviour, or let the system find people. On a small budget, going too narrow starves the system of the data it needs to learn — a reasonably broad audience with a sharp advert usually beats a clever narrow one.

THE CREATIVE IS MOST OF THE RESULT. On this channel the image or video and the first line do more than any setting. Stop the scroll, say one thing, make the next step obvious. A brilliantly targeted advert with a dull creative fails; a good creative on a broad audience often works.

TEST MESSAGES, NOT SETTINGS. Run two or three genuinely different messages against the same audience and let the results tell you which idea the market wants. Beginners endlessly adjust targeting while running one advert, and learn nothing.

ADDITION BEYOND THE VIDEO — three things that decide whether this works here. Build for a PHONE ON MOBILE DATA: vertical, captioned, light enough to load, and the message visible in the first line before anything expands. Send people where they will actually reply — for most small businesses that is a WHATSAPP CONVERSATION rather than a website, and an advert pointing anywhere nobody answers is money burnt. And watch LEAD QUALITY, not lead count: message campaigns can produce cheap conversations from people who were only curious, so count the enquiries that turn into customers and judge the cost against that.',
  practice_activity = 'Build one campaign with the objective closest to money for a real business, and write three genuinely different messages to test against the same audience. Check the creative on a phone on mobile data before launching. After it runs, count not just enquiries but how many became real customers, and calculate the cost per actual customer.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "Why is choosing the objective so consequential?", "options": ["The platform optimises hard for exactly what you ask for, so asking for engagement delivers people who will not buy", "It cannot be changed later", "It determines the minimum budget", "It sets the audience size"], "correct_index": 0}, {"question": "What should be tested on a small budget?", "options": ["Genuinely different messages against the same audience", "Many targeting combinations with one advert", "Different daily budgets", "Several objectives at once"], "correct_index": 0}]'::jsonb
where id = '40220912-eec0-4346-8bfe-101582beaaea';

-- Step-By-Step Google Ads Tutorial For Beginners
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Build a campaign with a tight structure rather than one large bucket', 'Choose keywords and match types deliberately', 'Stop paying for searches that will never buy'],
  notes = 'Structure is what separates a campaign that works from one that quietly wastes money, and it is decided before a shilling is spent.

ONE THEME PER AD GROUP. If a business does repairs, sales and accessories, that is three ad groups, each with its own keywords and its own adverts naming that thing. Relevance rises, cost per click falls, and you can actually tell what is working.

MATCH TYPES decide how loosely Google may interpret your keyword. Broad interpretation reaches far and wastes most; tighter matching shows your advert only for something close to what you typed. On a small budget, start tight and widen only when you have data. The default settings are generous to Google, not to you.

NEGATIVE KEYWORDS ARE THE HABIT THAT SAVES THE BUDGET. Look weekly at the actual searches that triggered your adverts. Add every irrelevant one to the negative list: free, jobs, salary, DIY, second hand, the name of a course. This single routine commonly saves a third of a small budget, and nobody does it consistently.

EVERY AD GROUP POINTS SOMEWHERE SPECIFIC. Not the homepage. The page about the thing the person searched for.

ADDITION BEYOND THE VIDEO — the local construction of a keyword list. Include place names, estates, landmarks and road names, because that is how people actually search here and those phrases are cheap. Include mixed English and Kiswahili phrasing where people use it. Expect genuinely small search volumes for a specific service in a specific town — that is not failure, it means a modest budget can own that search entirely, though the campaign will take longer to gather enough data to judge. And set the location targeting to where the business can actually serve, then check it is set to people IN that place rather than people merely interested in it; the default can quietly spend your budget abroad.',
  practice_activity = 'Build one campaign with at least three ad groups, each on a single theme with its own keywords and adverts pointing at a specific page. Write twenty keywords including place names and mixed-language phrasing, and twenty negative keywords before launching. After a week, read the actual search terms report and add every wasteful one to the negative list.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "What is the rule for ad group structure?", "options": ["One theme per ad group, with its own keywords, adverts and destination page", "As many keywords as possible in one group", "One ad group per campaign", "Group keywords by how much they cost"], "correct_index": 0}, {"question": "Which weekly routine most protects a small budget?", "options": ["Reading the actual search terms and adding wasteful ones as negative keywords", "Raising bids on the best keywords", "Pausing and restarting the campaign", "Adding more keywords"], "correct_index": 0}]'::jsonb
where id = '0b763e74-8be2-4d5e-a872-97e2931d7e22';

-- How To Setup Facebook Ads Targeting In 2026 (Step by Step)
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Build audiences from what a business already has rather than from guesses', 'Layer cold, warm and existing audiences deliberately', 'Avoid the targeting mistakes that waste a small budget'],
  notes = 'Targeting is three kinds of audience, and beginners only ever use the weakest one.

COLD: people who do not know the business, chosen by place, age and interest. This is the only one most beginners use, and it is the most expensive.

WARM: people who already engaged — watched the video, visited the page, messaged before. Far cheaper and far more likely to act, and it costs nothing to build except remembering to record it.

EXISTING CUSTOMERS, uploaded from the business''s own contact list. The most valuable audience there is, and also the basis for asking the platform to find more people who resemble them, which usually outperforms any interest targeting you could assemble by hand.

SO THE ORDER OF WORK IS: use what the business already has first, then look for people like them, and only then advertise to strangers by interest. Beginners do this exactly backwards.

DO NOT STACK INTERESTS UNTIL THE AUDIENCE IS TINY. A narrow audience on a small budget never gathers enough data for the system to learn, and you pay more per result for the privilege. Broad plus a sharp creative is usually better.

EXCLUDE deliberately: people who already bought, people who already enquired, and where relevant your own staff.

ADDITION BEYOND THE VIDEO — two things this market requires. First, there are rules about what may be uploaded and what may be targeted: a customer list must be data the business is entitled to use, and some categories of advertising carry extra restrictions. Kenya has a data protection law, so uploading a list of people who never agreed to hear from the business is a real risk, not a technicality. Second, TARGET WHERE THE BUSINESS CAN ACTUALLY DELIVER. An advert reaching the whole country for a shop that only delivers within one town spends most of its budget on people who cannot buy — and be careful with the setting for people ''recently in'' a place, which sweeps in travellers who are gone by the time you reply.',
  practice_activity = 'For one real business, build all three audience types: upload the customer list if they have one and are entitled to use it, create a warm audience from people who engaged, and define one cold audience. Then run the same advert against warm and cold and compare cost per enquiry. The gap is the lesson.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "What is the correct order of targeting work?", "options": ["Existing customers first, then people who resemble them, then strangers by interest", "Cold interest targeting first, since it reaches most people", "Whichever audience is largest", "Warm audiences only"], "correct_index": 0}, {"question": "What is the risk in uploading a customer list?", "options": ["It must be data the business is entitled to use, and Kenya''s data protection law applies", "Lists over a certain size are rejected", "It reveals the customers to competitors", "Uploaded lists cannot be excluded later"], "correct_index": 0}]'::jsonb
where id = 'd85ef223-9337-4d29-9380-e2bfddb5a76d';

-- How To Write Google Ads Copy That Converts
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Write adverts that match what the searcher typed', 'Use the limited space on what persuades rather than on description', 'Test copy properly instead of rewriting on instinct'],
  notes = 'Search advert copy has very little room and one job: convince someone that clicking this result answers the question they just typed.

MIRROR THE SEARCH. If someone typed ''fridge repair Thika'', the advert should say fridge repair in Thika. This is not a trick — the searcher is scanning for their own words, and seeing them is what earns the click. It also raises relevance, which lowers what you pay.

PUT THE DECIDING INFORMATION IN. Not adjectives. Price or price range, how fast, where you are, whether you come to them, what happens if it goes wrong. In a list of near-identical results, the specific one wins.

SAY WHAT TO DO. Call, book, get a quote today.

USE EVERY FIELD. The extra lines — location, phone number, links to specific pages, short callouts — take up more of the screen and give more reasons to click, and they are free. Ignoring them is leaving space to your competitors.

QUALIFY IN THE ADVERT. If the minimum job is a certain size, or you only serve one area, say so. An advert that prevents a wrong click has saved you money, which is the opposite of how beginners think about it.

TEST PROPERLY: run more than one advert per ad group, change ONE thing at a time, and wait for enough clicks before judging. Rewriting daily on instinct teaches you nothing and resets the learning.

ADDITION BEYOND THE VIDEO — three local specifics. Write the place the way people say it, including estates and landmarks. Mention M-PESA or PAY ON DELIVERY where it is true, because payment method is a genuine objection here and naming it removes a reason not to click. And make sure a CALL BUTTON is on the advert and that someone answers it: a large share of response here is a phone call rather than a form, and an unanswered number turns a paid click into a lost customer and a bad impression at once.',
  practice_activity = 'Write three different adverts for one ad group, each mirroring the search phrase and each carrying one piece of deciding information - price, speed, location or guarantee. Fill in every extra field available. Then run them and wait for enough clicks before judging, changing only one element between versions.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "Why should an advert mirror the words the person searched?", "options": ["The searcher scans for their own words, and matching raises relevance and lowers cost", "It is required by the platform", "It fills the character limit", "It improves the landing page score only"], "correct_index": 0}, {"question": "Why qualify in the advert by naming a minimum job or service area?", "options": ["An advert that prevents a wrong click has saved money", "It increases the click-through rate", "It is required for location targeting", "It makes the advert longer"], "correct_index": 0}]'::jsonb
where id = '245584dc-a480-42ea-830d-64659e123702';

-- Google Ads Bidding Optimization Strategies
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Improve a campaign that is already running, using its own data', 'Decide when to let the system bid automatically and when not to', 'Change one thing at a time so you can tell what worked'],
  notes = 'Module 3 covered how budget and bidding work. This lesson is about what to do once a campaign has been running and has data to learn from.

WAIT FOR ENOUGH DATA. The single most expensive habit in paid advertising is judging on too little. A handful of clicks tells you nothing; changing things daily means the campaign never settles and you never learn. Decide in advance how long a test runs and leave it alone.

AUTOMATED BIDDING NEEDS CONVERSIONS TO LEARN FROM. The system can bid better than you — but only once it has enough recorded conversions to see a pattern. A new campaign on a small budget with no tracking has none, so start manual or with a simple cost target, and move to automated bidding when there is a real history. Handing it to automation on day one with nothing to learn from is how small budgets disappear quietly.

WHAT TO ACT ON, in order: the search terms report, which shows what you are actually paying for; the keywords producing clicks and no conversions, which should be paused or bid down; the ones producing conversions, which deserve more; the times and places that convert; and the adverts that underperform.

AUTOMATED CAMPAIGN TYPES, where the platform decides placement and targeting for you, are worth knowing about and worth being careful with. They need volume and good tracking to work, they hide much of where the money went, and they will happily spend on the business''s own brand name and count it as success. Exclude the brand name, insist on proper tracking, and do not use them as a first campaign.

CHANGE ONE THING AT A TIME, and write down what you changed and when. Otherwise a campaign improves or collapses and you cannot say why — which means you cannot repeat it for the next client.

ADDITION BEYOND THE VIDEO — on a small budget the biggest wins are almost never in the bidding. They are in the negative keyword list, in sending clicks to a page that answers the search, and in someone answering the phone. Optimise those before touching a bid strategy, and keep a dated change log: it is what lets you tell a client exactly what you did and what it produced.',
  practice_activity = 'Take a campaign with at least two weeks of data and work in order: read the search terms report and add negatives, pause keywords with clicks and no conversions, and check which times and places convert. Change one thing, write it in a dated log, and wait before changing anything else.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "When should automated bidding be used?", "options": ["Once there is a real history of recorded conversions for it to learn from", "From the first day of every campaign", "Only on large budgets", "Never - manual bidding is always better"], "correct_index": 0}, {"question": "On a small budget, where are the biggest wins?", "options": ["Negative keywords, a matching destination page, and someone answering the phone", "The bid strategy", "The daily budget amount", "The number of campaigns"], "correct_index": 0}]'::jsonb
where id = 'f316e36e-4bc6-42ec-a601-d144388b1b41';
